## Focused review E2E probes: 28 October 2026

The [current user instruction](evidence/user-input-20261028-review-e2e-proof.txt) authorizes actual verification of review issues and tangible impact.
These proposed diagnostics investigate [review findings](10-issue-catalog.md#findings). They do not add approved business acceptance criteria.
The [case audit](evidence/review-e2e-20261028/case-audit.json) records exact source locators, predictions, and reachability limits.
Its SHA-256 is `56f1806c236867ed2de76bc1513be1e8d52df8fa1bf5e01d5cb81b76aeaddf88`.
All nine cases have definition version 1. Historical case sections above retain their earlier definitions and execution bindings.

### Review E2E fixture and observation points

| Item | Defined state or observation point |
| --- | --- |
| Comparison | Baseline `f025513f696476a9cc8162681410cbd21e029bcb`; candidate `e0030f5cf920b715f5831faf7df86cb9789b6d0f`. |
| Actor A and actor B | Separate synthetic local portal identities. Their credentials permit local Home access. |
| Session A and session B | Access, refresh, expiry, and identity observations attributed to the named login. Tokens remain excluded from retained evidence. |
| Browser | Installed Edge executes the actual source export, React components, reducers, and shared Axios handlers. |
| Public API action | The driver imports `@fm/base` and calls its exported Service or LoginUtil function inside the running page. |
| In-place login | Public `Service.postService` sends a fresh-login request while Home remains mounted. This is a controlled integration trigger. |
| Direct token replacement | Public `LoginUtil.setAuthorization` installs B access without the fresh-login request. This is a controlled consumer trigger. |
| Home | The portal tablist with accessible name `workspaces`. |
| Timeout dialog | Dialog named `Your session has been expired`; title ID `timeout-dialog-title`. |
| Extend and Logout | Buttons `Extend_Btn` and `Logout_Btn` inside the Timeout dialog. |
| PageLoader | Rendered element with test ID `MicroWebUI_base_Loader_Page`; pointer checks inspect the element above the target center. |
| Installed credentials | Compare hashed live `Hooks.getHooks().store` values and `localStorage.SET_TOKEN`. Record subject claims separately. |
| Acquisition | `POST /api/auth/v2/sso/refreshtoken`; route holds retain the request or response at a declared phase. |
| Renewal | `POST /api/auth/v2/sso/relogin`; capture request identity, provider result, installed access, and dialog disposition separately. |
| Validation | A read-only authentication-validation request carries the installed access and checks HTTP status plus JSON `result`. |
| Controlled clock | Driver clock advancement exercises recorded deadlines. It does not prove natural elapsed time or suspension. |
| Synthetic credentials | Opaque values and JWTs with omitted claims test rejection branches. They do not represent the inspected local BFF contract. |
| Isolation and cleanup | Separate browser contexts and output folders isolate cases. Release held routes and close owned contexts after retention. |

The inspected [Timeout UI](../../repos/ui/mfe-base/src/components/Timeout/index.tsx) supplies the dialog and button identities.
The [public exports](../../repos/ui/mfe-base/src/root.tsx) supply Service, Hooks, and LoginUtil.
The [runner inventory](evidence/review-e2e-20261028/runner-inventory.json) binds inspected dependencies and reusable setup sources.
Final assertions require served-source hashes at each comparison revision. Declared revisions alone do not bind executed bytes.

Execution at definition time: `NOT_RUN`. Actual results remain null until their matching execution is retained.
Retention target: `evidence/review-e2e-20261028/results.json`. This path is pending until the result matrix exists.
Each case must retain DOM states, credential hashes, correlated requests, screenshot hashes, and its exact assertion-source identity.
A reproduced branch is not a production incident. Production reachability remains limited by the case audit.

### REVIEW-E2E-01

Finding: [REVIEW-01](10-issue-catalog.md#review-01). Purpose: test the replacement-session dialog block.
Expected source comparison: baseline closes the old dialog and can clear B. Candidate preserves B but can retain the dialog.
The in-place public API login is synthetic. A reachable production UI trigger remains unknown.

```gherkin
@REVIEW-E2E-01
Feature: Observe dialog ownership after replacement login
  Scenario: Replacement login occurs during the old Timeout logout delay
    Given actor A has an expired access token and the Timeout dialog is visible
    When actor A clicks the button labeled "Logout" inside the Timeout dialog
    And the driver completes actor B login through the public API during the one-second logout delay
    And the driver advances the controlled clock beyond that delay
    Then candidate installed access equals the captured B access
    And the candidate Timeout dialog remains visible
    And the candidate button "Extend_Btn" is disabled inside the Timeout dialog
    When actor B clicks the button labeled "Logout" inside the candidate Timeout dialog
    Then no additional logout request starts
    And the candidate Timeout dialog remains visible
    And candidate installed access still equals B access
    And the baseline dialog and credential outcomes are retained separately
```

Tangible-impact predicate: a valid replacement session remains behind a dialog whose visible actions cannot recover access.
Owner: unknown application owner. Next action: confirm actual consumer reachability before assigning production impact.

### REVIEW-E2E-02

Finding: [REVIEW-02](10-issue-catalog.md#review-02). Purpose: compare rejected renewal-token shapes with a valid-token control.
Run separate opaque-access and missing-`iat` variants. The local BFF normally supplies usable `iat` and `exp` claims.
Synthetic response headers exercise client validation. They cannot prove that the real provider issues those token shapes.

```gherkin
@REVIEW-E2E-02
Feature: Observe renewal response validation
  Scenario Outline: A controlled renewal returns an unsupported access shape
    Given actor A has expired access and usable refresh credentials
    And the Timeout dialog is visible
    And the driver substitutes the renewal authorization header with "<shape>"
    When actor A clicks the button labeled "Extend" inside the Timeout dialog
    Then exactly one correlated relogin response is retained
    And the candidate does not install the substituted access value
    And the candidate Timeout dialog remains visible
    And the baseline installed-access and dialog outcomes are retained separately
    When the driver repeats renewal with a valid local provider access token
    Then the installed access equals that valid response token
    And the Timeout dialog is hidden
    And authentication validation uses the installed access and returns JSON "result" equal to true

    Examples:
      | shape |
      | opaque-access |
      | jwt-without-iat |
```

Tangible-impact predicate: malformed-token rejection prevents renewal in the disclosed fixture; the valid control must recover.
Owner: unknown authentication owner. Next action: bind the deployed provider token contract before treating this as a defect.

### REVIEW-E2E-03

Finding: [REVIEW-03](10-issue-catalog.md#review-03). Purpose: test recovery after a failed in-place fresh login.
Candidate source clears refresh credentials when the fresh-login request starts. Local active-session UI reachability remains unknown.

```gherkin
@REVIEW-E2E-03
Feature: Observe failed replacement-login recovery
  Scenario: A fresh login fails while session A remains active
    Given actor A has valid access A and usable refresh R_A
    And Home is visible
    When the driver sends an in-place fresh-login request through the public API
    And the controlled local response rejects that login
    Then candidate refresh state is empty
    And candidate installed access still equals A before its expiry
    And the failed login response and baseline credential state are retained separately
    When the driver advances the controlled clock beyond A expiry
    Then the Timeout dialog state and both button enabled states are retained
    And the result records whether a relogin request can start with R_A
    And the result records installed access and authentication-validation outcome after the attempted recovery
```

Tangible impact requires failed recovery after the login rejection. Refresh removal alone does not prove user interruption.
Owner: unknown application owner. Next action: identify an actual caller and compare its supported failure behavior.

### REVIEW-E2E-04

Finding: [REVIEW-04](10-issue-catalog.md#review-04). Purpose: test direct access replacement and the resulting renewal identity.
This probe calls the exported token utility. No local production caller for that direct replacement sequence is identified.

```gherkin
@REVIEW-E2E-04
Feature: Observe credential ownership after direct access replacement
  Scenario: A public token utility installs B access while A refresh remains
    Given actor A has usable access A and refresh R_A
    And actor B has separately acquired valid access B
    When the driver calls public LoginUtil.setAuthorization with B access
    Then candidate installed access equals B
    And candidate refresh state still equals R_A
    And baseline refresh state after the same replacement is retained
    When the driver requests renewal through the candidate public API
    Then the relogin request refresh credential hash equals the captured R_A hash
    And the response access subject and installed access subject are retained
    And the result records whether the installed identity changes from B to A
    And the visible Home or Timeout state is retained with the resulting credential hashes
```

Tangible-impact predicate: renewal changes the identity behind B access through retained A refresh.
Owner: unknown consumer owner. Next action: identify a supported direct-replacement caller before claiming production exposure.

### REVIEW-E2E-05

Finding: [REVIEW-05](10-issue-catalog.md#review-05). Purpose: test pending-acquisition controls and settlement behavior.
Hold the acquisition route at the recorded phase. Axios declares a 60-second timeout; disclose any clock advancement.

```gherkin
@REVIEW-E2E-05
Feature: Observe recovery while refresh acquisition is pending
  Scenario: Access expires while acquisition has not settled
    Given actor A has a correlated refresh acquisition request held by the driver
    When the driver advances the controlled clock beyond access expiry
    Then the candidate Timeout dialog is visible
    And the candidate button "Extend_Btn" is disabled inside the Timeout dialog
    And the candidate button "Logout_Btn" remains available inside the Timeout dialog
    And the acquisition request remains pending at that observation
    When the held acquisition settles through the recorded timeout or released failure
    Then acquisition settlement has a correlated request outcome
    And the candidate Timeout dialog, button states, and credential state are retained after settlement
    And the baseline pending and settled states are retained separately
```

Tangible impact is limited to the observed wait and available recovery. This probe cannot establish an indefinite wall-clock stall.
Owner: unknown application owner. Next action: assess natural suspended-browser timing only with a separate lifecycle run.

### REVIEW-E2E-06

Finding: [REVIEW-06](10-issue-catalog.md#review-06). Purpose: compare missing-refresh-expiry fallback behavior.
Inject a parseable synthetic refresh JWT without `exp`. The inspected local provider normally emits finite expiry.

```gherkin
@REVIEW-E2E-06
Feature: Observe fallback for an unusable refresh expiry
  Scenario: Access expires with a refresh token that has no expiry claim
    Given actor A has valid access and a parseable refresh JWT without "exp"
    When the driver advances the controlled clock beyond access expiry
    Then the candidate Timeout fallback starts logout
    And the candidate installed access becomes empty after logout completes
    And the candidate Sign In controls are visible
    And the logout request result and final refresh state are retained
    And the baseline dialog, Extend state, and installed access are retained separately
```

Tangible impact is logout in the malformed fixture. No occurrence with valid local-provider credentials is established.
Owner: unknown authentication owner. Next action: bind provider guarantees and the intended malformed-credential policy.

### REVIEW-E2E-07

Finding: [REVIEW-07](10-issue-catalog.md#review-07). Purpose: test recovery when Home registers inside the acquisition lead time.
Use usable refresh credentials and access with fewer than 25 seconds remaining when Home registers its timers.
Record the exact mounting or token-update path. Do not infer natural missed-deadline behavior from accelerated time.

```gherkin
@REVIEW-E2E-07
Feature: Observe near-expiry acquisition and completed recovery
  Scenario: Home registers timers with less than twenty-five seconds of access remaining
    Given actor A has usable refresh credentials
    When Home registers expiry handling with fewer than twenty-five seconds of access remaining
    Then the candidate does not start an immediate catch-up acquisition
    And the baseline acquisition request count is retained separately
    When the driver advances the controlled clock beyond access expiry
    And actor A clicks the button labeled "Extend" inside the Timeout dialog
    Then exactly one correlated relogin completes with usable local-provider access
    And installed access equals the returned access
    And the Timeout dialog is hidden
    And Home is visible
    And authentication validation uses the installed access and returns JSON "result" equal to true
```

Tangible-impact test: completed recovery disproves a recovery block for this usable-refresh fixture.
Owner: unknown application owner. Next action: keep any different registration or missing-refresh boundary separate.

### REVIEW-E2E-12

Finding: [REVIEW-12](10-issue-catalog.md#review-12). Purpose: measure visible loader behavior after stale request cleanup.
Use a held public Service loading request. Return a controlled HTTP 401 authentication-expiry response.
The actual error handler clears credentials and advances the epoch. The Login mount can publish a later loading cleanup.
This auth-error route differs from the earlier Timeout Logout hypothesis. Retain that boundary beside the result.

```gherkin
@REVIEW-E2E-12
Feature: Observe loader cleanup after authentication invalidation
  Scenario: An expired loading request settles after its session epoch changes
    Given actor A has valid access and visible Home
    And the shared loading state equals false
    When the driver starts a public Service loading request and holds its response
    Then shared loading state equals true
    And PageLoader is rendered in the portal
    When the driver releases a controlled HTTP 401 response with errorMessage "TOKEN_INVALID_EXPIRED"
    Then the original request rejects
    And installed access becomes empty
    And the result retains immediate shared loading and PageLoader states
    When at least two seconds of browser time pass after settlement
    Then the result retains the final Sign In visibility, shared loading state, and PageLoader visibility
    And a hit test records whether PageLoader covers the Sign In input center
    And a trial pointer action records whether that Sign In control is actionable
    And the result classifies persistent blocking only when the final loader and pointer evidence both support it
    And the baseline state transitions are retained separately
```

Tangible impact requires retained persistent pointer blocking. A transient loading state or registry return alone cannot establish it.
Owner: unknown application owner. Next action: compare this reachable error flow with any separately identified stale-completion caller.

### REVIEW-E2E-17

Finding: [REVIEW-17](10-issue-catalog.md#review-17). Purpose: separate provider validation time from delayed browser delivery.
The local BFF validates refresh credentials at request handling. Hold its successful response after that decision.
Advance browser time beyond the captured refresh deadline. This does not change provider policy or prove natural elapsed time.

```gherkin
@REVIEW-E2E-17
Feature: Observe successful renewal delivery after the refresh deadline
  Scenario: A provider accepts renewal before expiry and browser delivery occurs afterward
    Given actor A has expired access and usable refresh credentials before their recorded expiry
    And the Timeout dialog is visible
    When actor A clicks the button labeled "Extend" inside the Timeout dialog
    And the driver obtains a successful local relogin response before refresh expiry
    And the driver holds that response until browser time passes refresh expiry
    Then the candidate Timeout dialog remains visible while renewal is pending
    When the driver releases the successful response
    Then candidate installed access equals the accepted response access
    And the candidate Timeout dialog is hidden
    And Home is visible
    And authentication validation uses the installed access and returns JSON "result" equal to true
    And provider-decision time, response-delivery time, and baseline outcomes are retained separately
```

Tangible impact depends on the provider decision and final protected-access outcome. Late delivery alone does not establish unauthorized renewal.
Owner: unknown authentication owner. Next action: verify the selected provider policy before applying this result to deployment.
